  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current optimization strategies rely heavily on heuristic local searches that may get trapped in suboptimal solutions. This requires a systematic exploration through global optimization combined with exact relaxation methods. The key insight is that the minimum overlap problem can be formulated using convex programming by penalizing large correlation values with exponential functions. This ensures that the optimization aggressively targets peaks in the correlation curve while respecting the integral and domain constraints. \n\nCNN optimization shows superior performance for extremal optimization problems by exploiting dual variables to replace the max-function with a sum of penalty terms. We'll leverage CVXPY's automatic DCP transformations and MOSEK's exponential cone support to solve the resulting convex program.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe optimization targets a step function h:[0,2]\u21a6[0,1] with L2-normalization such that \n\u222ch(x)dx=1. The objective is to minimize\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222ch(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nThe penalty formulation is:\nmin_{h} \u2211\u2096 exp(-\u03b2(c\u2096 - \u03b1)) \nsubject to:\n  0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1, and\n  \u222ch(x)dx = 1\n\nConvex transformation:\nWe replace the exponential-max problem with a sum of exponential penalties via:\nf\u2081(h) = -\u03b1M + \u2211\u2096 exp(-\u03b2(c\u2096 - \u03b1))\n\nThe integral constraints are linear, while the domain bounds ensure box constraints. Gaussian convolutions smooth the solution while exponential cones handle the penalty terms.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize h as uniform distribution on [0,1] (n=140 samples)\n2. Precompute dx=2/140 and target=70\n3. Formulate CVXPY program:\n   - Variable: h \u2208 [-length 140]\n   - Objective: minimize sum(exp(-\u03b2(c\u2096 - \u03b1))) where c\u2096 is the correlation components\n   - Constraints: \n        for i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n        sum(h[i]*dx) \u2248 70\n\n4. Use exponential transformation: exp(-\u03b2|c\u2096 - \u03b1|) via CVXPY\n5. Solve with ECOS for the parent case and with MOSEK if available\n6. Tune \u03b1=0.38-0.42, \u03b2=500-10000\n7. Repair candidate if needed by projecting onto domain\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Base parameters: \u03b1=0.39, \u03b2=1000\n- Use heuristic to scale \u03b1/dx: s=\u03b1*dx\n- Try different lower bounds for the penalty function to prevent soft collapse\n- Random walk initialization\n- Progressive tightening of bounds with logarithmic scaling\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Solve CVXPY for 50 seconds\n- If time remains, iteratively refine starting from current solution\n- Repair any candidate by projecting onto [0,1] and normalizing\n- Fallback to sliding window optimization if CVXPY fails\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Verify \u222ch(x)dx\u224870 (|error|<1e-4)\n- Compute C\u2085 with same method as provided evaluator\n- Return(h_values, computed_C5, 140)\n\nThe exponential penalty provides globally enforced penalties for large correlations, unlike existing local optimizations. The convex formulation ensures the feasible set is properly explored, while exponential cones maintain all non-convexities can be approximated.",
